WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.227 --> 00:00:01.579
- Good morning Believe Nation.

00:00:01.579 --> 00:00:05.588
My name is Lily Ma and my
one word is extraordinary.

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:07.083
I believe that people are capable

00:00:07.083 --> 00:00:08.671
of doing extraordinary things,

00:00:08.671 --> 00:00:11.161
when they embrace who they are.

00:00:11.161 --> 00:00:14.030
So, today's message is, Have a Thick Skin.

00:00:14.030 --> 00:00:15.903
Over to you, Stephen Schwartzman.

00:00:15.903 --> 00:00:17.936
(rooster crowing)

00:00:17.936 --> 00:00:20.782
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:20.782 --> 00:00:23.532
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:00:23.532 --> 00:00:27.032
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:28.003 --> 00:00:29.761
- So for all of you people out there

00:00:29.761 --> 00:00:32.911
who want to be entrepreneurs,

00:00:32.911 --> 00:00:35.953
you got to have a thick skin.

00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:40.825
You almost need some friendly
psychologist training

00:00:40.825 --> 00:00:42.980
traipsing around with you.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.147
To buck you up from all
the setbacks that you have.

00:00:48.372 --> 00:00:51.292
It's always great after your Jack Ma,

00:00:51.292 --> 00:00:54.959
but it's not so great
if you file a chapter.

00:00:57.762 --> 00:01:00.780
And the difference in that, Jack's a 10.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.780
He almost did go down several times.

00:01:05.039 --> 00:01:07.349
It's that scrambling ability,

00:01:07.349 --> 00:01:11.516
the ability to take that
kind of abuse and rejection

00:01:12.521 --> 00:01:14.840
and believe in your basic plan,

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.923
but be adaptable and just keep going.

00:01:18.892 --> 00:01:20.200
- So one of the most important things

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.693
I'm working on right now is
to be a great storyteller.

00:01:23.693 --> 00:01:25.881
So, I wanted to
incorporate telling stories

00:01:25.881 --> 00:01:28.862
in the classes I teach
at Toronto Dance Salsa.

00:01:28.862 --> 00:01:32.963
And I started telling stories
about my husband, my parents,

00:01:32.963 --> 00:01:36.472
and started to really
show my vulnerable side.

00:01:36.472 --> 00:01:40.792
And then I got the most
brutal feedback I've ever had

00:01:40.792 --> 00:01:43.372
in the entire time that
I have taught here.

00:01:43.372 --> 00:01:45.092
So he said that I spend too much time

00:01:45.092 --> 00:01:48.271
talking about aimless, personal stuff.

00:01:48.271 --> 00:01:52.084
And immediately, I became very defensive

00:01:52.084 --> 00:01:54.290
because I was really hurt.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.031
You know, I didn't want to think

00:01:56.031 --> 00:01:58.770
that stories about my parents

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.932
and about my husband as
aimless, personal stuff.

00:02:01.932 --> 00:02:03.932
And I just started to think,

00:02:03.932 --> 00:02:05.572
oh well, you know, this
person doesn't know

00:02:05.572 --> 00:02:07.183
what they're talking about.

00:02:07.183 --> 00:02:09.243
They don't know about story-telling.

00:02:09.243 --> 00:02:11.104
Whatever. They could go somewhere else.

00:02:11.104 --> 00:02:13.095
I don't need them in my class.

00:02:13.095 --> 00:02:15.794
But that all came from a place of pain.

00:02:15.794 --> 00:02:18.544
Because I knew, deep down inside,

00:02:20.014 --> 00:02:22.395
that I wasn't good at telling stories.

00:02:22.395 --> 00:02:23.974
I did take too long.

00:02:23.974 --> 00:02:27.114
I did tell things that probably did not

00:02:27.114 --> 00:02:28.214
relate to the students.

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:30.794
But I couldn't see it past the hurt

00:02:30.794 --> 00:02:33.546
and I just felt really defensive.

00:02:33.546 --> 00:02:36.543
So, as a result of that pain
and that harsh criticism,

00:02:36.543 --> 00:02:38.564
I just stopped telling stories altogether.

00:02:38.564 --> 00:02:40.134
And that wasn't the answer either

00:02:40.134 --> 00:02:43.911
because I really want
to tell great stories.

00:02:43.911 --> 00:02:46.343
And then I struggled
with it for a long time.

00:02:46.343 --> 00:02:49.281
And I wasn't sure what to do.

00:02:49.281 --> 00:02:53.985
And then one day, I saw
a quote from Brene Brown.

00:02:53.985 --> 00:02:55.024
The quote said,

00:02:55.024 --> 00:02:59.244
"When you're brave enough, you will fall."

00:02:59.244 --> 00:03:03.755
And immediately, I felt like
this was just a fall for me.

00:03:03.755 --> 00:03:07.572
And I realized that this dream
of mine is very important

00:03:07.572 --> 00:03:10.756
and I needed to be brave to continue on.

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:13.089
So I decided to get feedback

00:03:14.264 --> 00:03:17.544
and learn about how to tell better stories

00:03:17.544 --> 00:03:19.155
and practice it even more in class,

00:03:19.155 --> 00:03:21.254
even though I was so scared of

00:03:21.254 --> 00:03:23.016
getting that harsh criticism again.

00:03:23.016 --> 00:03:24.384
I kept on going.

00:03:24.384 --> 00:03:26.388
And eventually I got better.

00:03:26.388 --> 00:03:28.449
So I decided to shift my perspective.

00:03:28.449 --> 00:03:32.347
I decided to see feedback
as an opportunity for growth

00:03:32.347 --> 00:03:34.588
versus a personal attack.

00:03:34.588 --> 00:03:39.119
So now, every time I get
feedback, I apply it right away.

00:03:39.119 --> 00:03:42.689
So that way I can grow and
become a more confident speaker.

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:44.169
I still have a long way to go

00:03:44.169 --> 00:03:46.218
but I'm getting better each day.

00:03:46.218 --> 00:03:50.267
So next time, when your faced
with criticism or failure,

00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:51.736
shift your perspective.

00:03:51.736 --> 00:03:53.997
If this dream is important enough for you,

00:03:53.997 --> 00:03:56.188
you have to be brave enough to pursue it.

00:03:56.188 --> 00:03:58.040
So the question of the day is:

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.500
What is that one harsh
criticism that you received

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.328
and now by shifting perspective,

00:04:03.328 --> 00:04:04.907
you were able to learn from it?

00:04:04.907 --> 00:04:06.367
So leave in the comments below

00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:08.117
and I will join in on the discussion.

00:04:08.117 --> 00:04:10.517
I also have a quick shout
out from Michael Lardizabal.

00:04:10.517 --> 00:04:11.359
Thank you so much, Michael,

00:04:11.359 --> 00:04:13.517
for picking up a copy of Evan's book.

00:04:13.517 --> 00:04:14.900
Your support means a lot to us

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.546
and I hope your enjoying the read.

00:04:16.546 --> 00:04:18.437
Thank you so much for watching.

00:04:18.437 --> 00:04:19.460
We believe in you

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:21.936
and I hope you continue
to believe in yourself

00:04:21.936 --> 00:04:24.069
and whatever your one word is.

00:04:24.069 --> 00:04:25.958
And we'll see you again tomorrow morning

00:04:25.958 --> 00:04:28.419
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:04:28.419 --> 00:04:31.131
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:04:31.131 --> 00:04:34.548
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:41.602
- The best defense to
speech that you don't like

00:04:41.602 --> 00:04:43.811
about yourself as a public figure

00:04:43.811 --> 00:04:46.142
is to develop a thick skin.

00:04:46.142 --> 00:04:49.372
It's really the only effective defense

00:04:49.372 --> 00:04:51.441
because you can't stop it.

00:04:51.441 --> 00:04:53.137
You know, you are going
to be misunderstood.

00:04:53.137 --> 00:04:55.734
If you're doing anything
interesting in the world,

00:04:55.734 --> 00:04:57.316
you're going to have critics.

00:04:57.316 --> 00:05:00.505
The only way, if you absolutely
can't tolerate critics,

00:05:00.505 --> 00:05:04.396
then don't do anything new or interesting.

00:05:04.396 --> 00:05:06.575
And then you can insulate yourself.

00:05:06.575 --> 00:05:08.256
Then think how wonderful
your life will be.

00:05:08.256 --> 00:05:09.484
If you see something, I don't know,

00:05:09.484 --> 00:05:11.400
you're kind of a public figure,

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.799
things have probably
been written about you

00:05:12.799 --> 00:05:13.958
that you didn't think were nice.

00:05:13.958 --> 00:05:14.791
- That's true.

00:05:14.791 --> 00:05:18.718
- And my advice, if you
came to me and said,

00:05:18.718 --> 00:05:20.190
"Jeff, this, you know, somebody wrote this

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.704
and it really hurt my feelings.

00:05:21.704 --> 00:05:23.029
What should I do?"

00:05:23.029 --> 00:05:26.446
I would say, "Go stand on a street corner

00:05:28.344 --> 00:05:29.278
in a crowded urban area,

00:05:29.278 --> 00:05:31.739
and watch all the people walk by

00:05:31.739 --> 00:05:33.907
and think about what
they're thinking about.

00:05:33.907 --> 00:05:36.717
I bet you none of those people
are thinking about you."

00:05:36.717 --> 00:05:39.227
Really, in your mind, you can
do this thought experiment.

00:05:39.227 --> 00:05:41.239
Like, okay, there's a
woman who just walked by.

00:05:41.239 --> 00:05:42.527
What's she actually thinking about?

00:05:42.527 --> 00:05:45.557
Probably maybe what she's going to cook

00:05:45.557 --> 00:05:46.666
for dinner that night,

00:05:46.666 --> 00:05:51.267
or the argument that she had
with one of her employees,

00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:54.855
or whatever it is, it's not about us.

00:05:54.855 --> 00:05:57.397
- Who is an entrepreneur where
everything is a smooth ride

00:05:57.397 --> 00:05:59.208
and you go through these
things and I'll tell you

00:05:59.208 --> 00:06:01.664
and you'll get criticized and
you have to have a thick skin.

00:06:01.664 --> 00:06:02.497
Yeah.

00:06:02.497 --> 00:06:03.719
(laughing)

00:06:03.719 --> 00:06:04.552
- [Interviewer] You've been there?

00:06:04.552 --> 00:06:05.385
- I've been there.

00:06:05.385 --> 00:06:08.700
And by the way, you don't buy a thick skin

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.238
and you don't even know you have it

00:06:10.238 --> 00:06:12.799
until somebody starts
to try to puncture it.

00:06:12.799 --> 00:06:14.370
And then you discover what you're made of.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:17.301
I also heard that you went to a school

00:06:17.301 --> 00:06:20.630
that was out of your initial neighborhood.

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:22.851
- Yeah, I was bused to school.

00:06:22.851 --> 00:06:25.684
It was pretty, you know, whatever.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:31.297
People were mean, and they
called you nigger and whatever,

00:06:31.297 --> 00:06:35.464
and spit on you and threw feces
and piss and whatever at you

00:06:36.806 --> 00:06:38.307
and you know.

00:06:38.307 --> 00:06:39.476
It is what it is.

00:06:39.476 --> 00:06:40.624
There's nothing I can do about it.

00:06:40.624 --> 00:06:43.108
It made me stronger.

00:06:43.108 --> 00:06:45.441
Um, I'm not here without it.

00:06:48.248 --> 00:06:52.165
You can't phase me with
meanness because of it.

00:06:53.298 --> 00:06:57.465
So, you know, that was my
grade school experience.

00:06:58.868 --> 00:07:01.090
Grade school, high school,
junior high school.

